launchSettings.json is a development-only configuration file that tells Visual Studio, Rider,
and the dotnet run CLI how to start your application — which URLs to listen on, 
which environment to set, and which browser to open. It never gets deployed to production.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MyApp/
├── Properties/
│   └── launchSettings.json    ← lives here, dev only
├── Controllers/
├── appsettings.json           ← runtime config, deployed
├── appsettings.Development.json
└── Program.cs
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/launchsettings.json",

  "iisSettings": {                          // IIS Express settings
    "windowsAuthentication": false,
    "anonymousAuthentication": true,
    "iisExpress": {
      "applicationUrl": "http://localhost:52345",
      "sslPort": 44321
    }
  },

  "profiles": {                             // one entry per launch profile
    "http":        { "..." },             // Kestrel HTTP (modern default)
    "https":       { "..." },             // Kestrel HTTPS (modern default)
    "IIS Express": { "..." },             // auto-added by VS on Windows
    "Docker":      { "..." }              // optional Docker profile
  }
}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Kestrel Profile (primary — works everywhere)
"https": {
  "commandName": "Project",             // launch via Kestrel (dotnet run)
  "dotnetRunMessages": true,           // show dotnet run output
  "launchBrowser": true,
  "launchUrl": "openapi/v1.json",    // .NET 9+ default (was "swagger" pre-.NET 9)
  "applicationUrl": "https://localhost:7234;http://localhost:5147",
  "environmentVariables": {
    "ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"
  }
}
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IIS Express Profile (Visual Studio on Windows only)
"IIS Express": {
  "commandName": "IISExpress",         // launch via IIS Express
  "launchBrowser": true,
  "launchUrl": "openapi/v1.json",
  "environmentVariables": {
    "ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"
  }
}
// ↑ Auto-added by Visual Studio on Windows.
//   On macOS / Linux / VS Code / Rider — you'll never see this profile.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
applicationUrl — Controlling Dev Ports 🔗
The applicationUrl property in a profile tells Kestrel exactly which URLs and ports to listen on during development.
You can specify multiple URLs separated by a semicolon — Kestrel will bind to all of them simultaneously.

Single vs Multiple URLs
// Single URL — HTTP only
"applicationUrl": "http://localhost:5000"
 
// Multiple URLs — HTTPS + HTTP (Kestrel binds both)
"applicationUrl": "https://localhost:7001;http://localhost:5000"
 
// Bind to all interfaces (accessible from other devices on LAN)
"applicationUrl": "https://0.0.0.0:7001;http://0.0.0.0:5000"
